00:02:03.670 --> 00:02:07.640
A quick history-- and
I've lost things, here.

00:02:07.640 --> 00:02:09.280
Hold on.

00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:09.850
There we go.

00:02:09.850 --> 00:02:14.700
Before we had Swagger, basically
the state-of-the-art was WSDL

00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:15.730
and WADL.

00:02:15.730 --> 00:02:19.870
Those were the ways you could
describe services, really.

00:02:19.870 --> 00:02:22.510
They were generally
HTTP-based services,

00:02:22.510 --> 00:02:24.220
and then in 2010,
a company called

00:02:24.220 --> 00:02:28.540
Wordnik started building
their own tooling that would

00:02:28.540 --> 00:02:30.550
eventually be called Swagger.

00:02:30.550 --> 00:02:32.860
And that's part of what
made this description

00:02:32.860 --> 00:02:35.290
format that they'd put into
what they were doing exciting.

00:02:35.290 --> 00:02:37.830
It had authenticity.

00:02:37.830 --> 00:02:39.612
This wasn't a
vendor saying, this

00:02:39.612 --> 00:02:41.320
is the way everyone
should describe APIs.

00:02:41.320 --> 00:02:43.600
This was someone saying,
hey, we need to do this.

00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:45.500
And then over time, as
more and more people,

00:02:45.500 --> 00:02:47.541
they open-sourced it, and
as more and more people

00:02:47.541 --> 00:02:49.990
got involved, they
had to add things.

00:02:49.990 --> 00:02:52.380
In the beginning, it was
just a plain old API key.

00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:55.420
But not all APIs
just use API keys.

00:02:55.420 --> 00:02:57.460
So over time, as the
community asked for things

00:02:57.460 --> 00:02:59.810
and they saw this organic
adoption and growth that

00:02:59.810 --> 00:03:02.290
happened, and then
in 2014-- so Apigee

00:03:02.290 --> 00:03:05.740
had been watching this go on.

00:03:05.740 --> 00:03:07.780
We liked what we
saw with Swagger,

00:03:07.780 --> 00:03:10.200
but it wasn't quite
descriptive enough for us.

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:13.030
We needed a few things and
a few changes to the spec,

00:03:13.030 --> 00:03:17.050
and so we approached Tony,
and Wordnik became Reverb

00:03:17.050 --> 00:03:18.130
at that point.

00:03:18.130 --> 00:03:20.320
And we asked if
they were interested

00:03:20.320 --> 00:03:21.477
in the next generation.

00:03:21.477 --> 00:03:22.060
They said yes.

00:03:22.060 --> 00:03:24.324
So we added a bunch of
really cool things to that.

00:03:24.324 --> 00:03:26.680
And that process
took about six months

00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:28.750
to get from the beginning
of the working group

00:03:28.750 --> 00:03:31.750
to the version 2 spec.

00:03:31.750 --> 00:03:35.370
And then after that, companies
shuffled around a bit.

00:03:35.370 --> 00:03:38.200
And then in 2015,
about a year after we

00:03:38.200 --> 00:03:41.590
started talking about how
Swagger could become neutrally

00:03:41.590 --> 00:03:44.316
governed, maybe under
The Linux Foundation,

00:03:44.316 --> 00:03:45.190
it actually happened.

00:03:45.190 --> 00:03:46.690
So at the end of 2015--

00:03:46.690 --> 00:03:49.210
I think it was November--

00:03:49.210 --> 00:03:50.950
the Open API Initiative
was officially

00:03:50.950 --> 00:03:52.420
founded and launched.

00:03:52.420 --> 00:03:57.130
And SmartBear, who had acquired
the interests for Swagger,

00:03:57.130 --> 00:03:59.560
contributed the specification.

00:03:59.560 --> 00:04:02.800
So not the Swagger
universal brand.

00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:04.570
That they broke out
the specification

00:04:04.570 --> 00:04:06.770
and they gave that to
the Open API Initiative.

00:04:09.610 --> 00:04:11.650
Within a couple of
months after that,

00:04:11.650 --> 00:04:16.060
we began what was the Version
3 iteration of the spec.

00:04:16.060 --> 00:04:19.675
We created a Technical
Developer Community,

00:04:19.675 --> 00:04:20.800
and there were six members.

00:04:20.800 --> 00:04:22.050
I'll talk about that in a bit.

00:04:24.420 --> 00:04:27.770
But this is really why
we started to get excited

00:04:27.770 --> 00:04:30.440
about things and why it's so
important that the spec is

00:04:30.440 --> 00:04:32.010
neutrally governed.

00:04:32.010 --> 00:04:35.180
So-- and this is just one
of several different metrics

00:04:35.180 --> 00:04:36.560
that all show the same thing.

00:04:36.560 --> 00:04:39.334
We saw downloads going up.

00:04:39.334 --> 00:04:41.750
If you look at Google Trends,
you could see similar lines,

00:04:41.750 --> 00:04:43.400
basically.

00:04:43.400 --> 00:04:48.310
So here is where the Swagger 2
spec working group kicked off.

00:04:48.310 --> 00:04:51.770
That's about when it became
the official spec and tooling

00:04:51.770 --> 00:04:52.880
started to catch up.

00:04:52.880 --> 00:04:54.546
And then you can see
a little further up

00:04:54.546 --> 00:04:57.571
the graph is when the Open API
Initiative happened, and then

00:04:57.571 --> 00:04:58.070
today.

00:04:58.070 --> 00:04:59.310
We're at the far right.

00:04:59.310 --> 00:05:01.226
And that's where the
version 3 of the spec is.

00:05:04.060 --> 00:05:06.170
So why did this matter?

00:05:06.170 --> 00:05:08.390
Why was it so
important to put this

00:05:08.390 --> 00:05:10.640
into some neutral
governance model?

00:05:10.640 --> 00:05:14.030
Well, really it
was to protect it.

00:05:16.940 --> 00:05:18.410
The industry needed
a standard way

00:05:18.410 --> 00:05:21.590
to describe APIs
and lots of vendors

00:05:21.590 --> 00:05:24.860
wanted a way to be able to share
these kinds of descriptions,

00:05:24.860 --> 00:05:25.990
and so it mattered.

00:05:25.990 --> 00:05:29.390
But while that specification was
the property of one company--

00:05:29.390 --> 00:05:30.940
it's too important
for one company

00:05:30.940 --> 00:05:32.240
to have that kind of control.

00:05:32.240 --> 00:05:34.820
And so putting this into
the Open API Initiative

00:05:34.820 --> 00:05:36.970
under The Linux Foundation
was really important,

00:05:36.970 --> 00:05:37.790
and it matters.

00:05:37.790 --> 00:05:41.150
One of the challenges
is that it's

00:05:41.150 --> 00:05:43.160
hard to not call it Swagger.

00:05:43.160 --> 00:05:45.800
We have a mark that
describes this service.

00:05:45.800 --> 00:05:48.350
It's OpenAPI.

00:05:48.350 --> 00:05:51.920
And Swagger is a trademark that
applies to a brand of tools

00:05:51.920 --> 00:05:54.474
that SmartBear operates.

00:05:54.474 --> 00:05:55.890
And they do a great
job with that.

00:05:55.890 --> 00:05:56.973
They have a big community.

00:05:56.973 --> 00:05:59.780
That's what the specification
is known for first.

00:05:59.780 --> 00:06:02.900
But having the name OpenAPI
and using the name OpenAPI

00:06:02.900 --> 00:06:03.930
is really important.

00:06:03.930 --> 00:06:06.410
So if you're building support
for this into your products,

00:06:06.410 --> 00:06:07.891
you should use the OpenAPI name.

00:06:07.891 --> 00:06:08.390
It matters.

00:06:14.200 --> 00:06:16.330
In the beginning, there
were nine members.

00:06:16.330 --> 00:06:18.850
Two of those were
Apigee and Google.

00:06:18.850 --> 00:06:21.740
There were several
others, as well.

00:06:21.740 --> 00:06:24.970
But that's in part how I--
it's interesting to me,

00:06:24.970 --> 00:06:27.975
because that's how I began to
meet people at Google, totally

00:06:27.975 --> 00:06:29.600
oblivious to the fact
that at one point

00:06:29.600 --> 00:06:31.540
Apigee would become
part of Google

00:06:31.540 --> 00:06:35.110
and I'd actually work with
people like [INAUDIBLE].

00:06:35.110 --> 00:06:38.850
And I think it's pretty exciting
that we went from nine members,

00:06:38.850 --> 00:06:41.890
and over the time since it
launched at the end of 2015,

00:06:41.890 --> 00:06:42.980
we're now up to 21.

00:06:42.980 --> 00:06:46.090
And when I made this slide
first two weeks ago, it was 20,

00:06:46.090 --> 00:06:47.200
and then was 21.

00:06:47.200 --> 00:06:50.281
I have to keep updating it.

00:06:50.281 --> 00:06:52.780
But I think it's pretty exciting
and a great show of support

00:06:52.780 --> 00:06:55.262
across the industry that we
see all of this happening.

00:06:59.200 --> 00:07:02.290
One of the interesting bits
and sort of OpenAPI trivia

00:07:02.290 --> 00:07:07.130
is that there is a space in
the name of the initiative,

00:07:07.130 --> 00:07:08.660
the Open API Initiative.

00:07:08.660 --> 00:07:10.584
And so you can see that
in the style guide.

00:07:10.584 --> 00:07:12.375
But the spec actually
doesn't have a space.

00:07:12.375 --> 00:07:15.410
And this is the fun trivia
fact you can impress people

00:07:15.410 --> 00:07:18.351
at parties with, is that
we realized a bit too late,

00:07:18.351 --> 00:07:20.600
after the charter had already
been drafted and signed,

00:07:20.600 --> 00:07:23.750
that it's a lot easier
to Google "OpenAPI"

00:07:23.750 --> 00:07:26.155
without a space than
"Open API" with a space.

00:07:26.155 --> 00:07:28.280
But it was a little too
late to change the charter,

00:07:28.280 --> 00:07:29.446
and so we just went with it.

00:07:29.446 --> 00:07:32.120
It actually has some
interesting side benefits.

00:07:32.120 --> 00:07:33.857
If people use it
incorrectly, it's

00:07:33.857 --> 00:07:35.690
a good sign that they
haven't paid attention

00:07:35.690 --> 00:07:39.110
to the details of the spec, so
I think that makes it a feature

00:07:39.110 --> 00:07:40.010
if you document it.

00:07:45.300 --> 00:07:49.872
So your first takeaway
should be that there

00:07:49.872 --> 00:07:51.830
are lots of ways, however
you look at it, where

00:07:51.830 --> 00:07:54.470
you can say that OpenAPI
has emerged as the industry

00:07:54.470 --> 00:07:55.550
standard.

00:07:55.550 --> 00:07:59.743
It is the most common way
to describe "RESTful"--

00:07:59.743 --> 00:08:01.761
I'll put that in quotes--

00:08:01.761 --> 00:08:02.260
APIs.

00:08:05.870 --> 00:08:07.820
So moving on, why
specs actually matter.

00:08:07.820 --> 00:08:10.730
Because having a specification
[? way ?] to describe,

00:08:10.730 --> 00:08:11.330
that's great.

00:08:11.330 --> 00:08:14.180
But without tooling and how
that fits into your workflow,

00:08:14.180 --> 00:08:15.960
it makes things
better-- without that,

00:08:15.960 --> 00:08:17.260
there's really no point to it.

00:08:17.260 --> 00:08:19.940
So in order to understand
the spec and why it matters,

00:08:19.940 --> 00:08:21.606
we have to talk about
the tooling a bit.

00:08:23.960 --> 00:08:27.020
There are many ways in
which specs help you

00:08:27.020 --> 00:08:28.820
in your API lifecycle.

00:08:28.820 --> 00:08:32.659
I'm going to cover seven that
we've used in our workflows

00:08:32.659 --> 00:08:35.508
at Apigee at different
points along the way,

00:08:35.508 --> 00:08:36.799
but there are plenty of others.

00:08:36.799 --> 00:08:38.132
I just picked my favorite seven.

00:08:41.360 --> 00:08:44.059
One, just simply having a
formal, machine-readable

00:08:44.059 --> 00:08:45.980
interface description
or definition--

00:08:45.980 --> 00:08:49.160
and I'll talk about that
on point 7, the distinction

00:08:49.160 --> 00:08:52.070
between those two.

00:08:52.070 --> 00:08:55.130
It matters because one,
it's language neutral.

00:08:55.130 --> 00:08:58.104
So maybe your polyglot--

00:08:58.104 --> 00:09:00.395
you're building services in
lots of different languages

00:09:00.395 --> 00:09:02.686
and you need one common way
to describe those services.

00:09:05.070 --> 00:09:09.080
And since the format for
OpenAPI is YAML or JSON,

00:09:09.080 --> 00:09:11.570
because YAML's a
superset of JSON,

00:09:11.570 --> 00:09:14.540
it gives you a way to have
consistency across teams that

00:09:14.540 --> 00:09:15.200
really isn't--

00:09:15.200 --> 00:09:18.260
since everyone has to deal
with serializing data anyway,

00:09:18.260 --> 00:09:20.250
they're all used to that.

00:09:20.250 --> 00:09:24.080
Two is that once you can
describe it for machines,

00:09:24.080 --> 00:09:25.010
you can validate it.

00:09:25.010 --> 00:09:28.030
So now you can make sure that
mistakes happen less often.

00:09:28.030 --> 00:09:31.040
So the ability to validate
is really important.

00:09:31.040 --> 00:09:36.210
But mostly we used to make
50-plus page Google Docs,

00:09:36.210 --> 00:09:39.590
or before that, Word
docs, where you'd

00:09:39.590 --> 00:09:41.769
describe all this
interface, you'd

00:09:41.769 --> 00:09:44.060
hand it to an engineering
team, they'd build something,

00:09:44.060 --> 00:09:46.940
give it back to you,
and maybe it matched.

00:09:46.940 --> 00:09:49.262
Once you have a
machine-readable spec upfront,

00:09:49.262 --> 00:09:50.720
if you do that in
the design phase,

00:09:50.720 --> 00:09:55.262
you can everybody that can
drive agreement across teams.

00:09:55.262 --> 00:09:56.470
Which brings us to point two.

00:09:56.470 --> 00:10:01.180
So if you're treating that
specification in your design

00:10:01.180 --> 00:10:05.830
phase as the contract, now
you can let both the client

00:10:05.830 --> 00:10:07.780
and the server-side--
we can get people

00:10:07.780 --> 00:10:11.410
to agree on what actually
is being built and why.

00:10:11.410 --> 00:10:14.450
Because you never get your API
design right the first time.

00:10:14.450 --> 00:10:16.060
You're going to have
to change things,

00:10:16.060 --> 00:10:22.520
and the faster you can
do that, the better.

00:10:22.520 --> 00:10:24.567
Oh, I forgot one last
key point on this.

00:10:24.567 --> 00:10:26.900
The machine-readable interface
description is ultimately

00:10:26.900 --> 00:10:31.192
going to mean less
work, and less work

00:10:31.192 --> 00:10:32.150
is going to be a theme.

00:10:32.150 --> 00:10:33.600
You're going to hear that
on every one of these--

00:10:33.600 --> 00:10:34.516
spoiler alert--

00:10:34.516 --> 00:10:35.640
every one of these reasons.

00:10:35.640 --> 00:10:37.180
It's because it's less work.

00:10:37.180 --> 00:10:41.129
So having that contract
and allowing teams

00:10:41.129 --> 00:10:43.170
to have discussions about
what these services are

00:10:43.170 --> 00:10:45.170
and how they work, that matters.

00:10:45.170 --> 00:10:50.970
It ends up you get better
APIs and you do less work.

00:10:50.970 --> 00:10:53.610
You can use those specs to
even drive a mock service.

00:10:53.610 --> 00:10:57.390
So now, instead of waiting
for the server team

00:10:57.390 --> 00:11:00.929
to stand up a version of the
API, if you can drive a mock,

00:11:00.929 --> 00:11:02.970
you can have the client
team working in parallel.

00:11:02.970 --> 00:11:05.130
You can begin to explore
how this is actually

00:11:05.130 --> 00:11:08.010
going to work with the client
applications that are deriving

00:11:08.010 --> 00:11:10.445
value from that API.

00:11:10.445 --> 00:11:12.570
It also means you can learn
faster, because now you

00:11:12.570 --> 00:11:14.430
can validate what you got
right or wrong in the design

00:11:14.430 --> 00:11:16.260
before a line of code
has been written.

00:11:16.260 --> 00:11:17.980
And again, you do less work.

00:11:20.540 --> 00:11:25.940
You can use that spec to drive
client SDK-generation or server

00:11:25.940 --> 00:11:29.175
generations, server-side stubs.

00:11:29.175 --> 00:11:30.800
This is good because
you end up getting

00:11:30.800 --> 00:11:32.258
more consistent
interfaces when you

00:11:32.258 --> 00:11:35.090
start treating that spec
like code in this way.

00:11:35.090 --> 00:11:37.040
Then you end up
getting things that

00:11:37.040 --> 00:11:39.990
are more likely to agree with
the spec that you started with.

00:11:39.990 --> 00:11:43.942
There are fewer
mistakes and less work.

00:11:43.942 --> 00:11:45.650
You guys can follow
along when I do that.

00:11:45.650 --> 00:11:48.170
I'll give you the cue.

00:11:48.170 --> 00:11:50.990
So, generally
speaking, if you're

00:11:50.990 --> 00:11:53.150
treating your spec
as code, then you

00:11:53.150 --> 00:11:56.850
have it under source control,
and you can track changes.

00:11:56.850 --> 00:11:59.330
This can be really great, for
instance-- your tech writers.

00:11:59.330 --> 00:12:00.830
If you want your
tech writers to be

00:12:00.830 --> 00:12:02.435
able to keep up with what's
changing in the interface,

00:12:02.435 --> 00:12:03.268
there's a great way.

00:12:03.268 --> 00:12:08.510
You can just look at the
commit history for the spec.

00:12:08.510 --> 00:12:10.880
So by empowering them,
you have fewer mistakes,

00:12:10.880 --> 00:12:13.928
you've got better
documentation, and you've done--

00:12:13.928 --> 00:12:15.330
ah, this is good.

00:12:15.330 --> 00:12:16.270
You've got it.

00:12:16.270 --> 00:12:18.970
Number six you'll do better.

00:12:18.970 --> 00:12:21.640
You can also make
your writers very

00:12:21.640 --> 00:12:23.980
happy by being able to
generate docs, and maybe even

00:12:23.980 --> 00:12:25.040
interactive docs.

00:12:25.040 --> 00:12:29.110
You can also generate
tests from that spec.

00:12:29.110 --> 00:12:31.462
We did that with some of
our node-based applications,

00:12:31.462 --> 00:12:32.920
if you ever play
with Swagger Node.

00:12:32.920 --> 00:12:34.510
And I'll talk about
that in a bit.

00:12:34.510 --> 00:12:37.009
It actually has something called
Swagger Test Templates that

00:12:37.009 --> 00:12:38.971
was folded right into that.

00:12:38.971 --> 00:12:41.470
I think this is a great area
of opportunity in tooling, too.

00:12:41.470 --> 00:12:44.890
I think if you are interested
in generating tests

00:12:44.890 --> 00:12:48.310
from your specs, that there are
a bunch of folks out there also

00:12:48.310 --> 00:12:50.930
looking for a really good
solution in that way.

00:12:50.930 --> 00:12:54.190
So again, you end up with
docs that are more accurate,

00:12:54.190 --> 00:12:56.470
they have fewer mistakes,
you can use tests

00:12:56.470 --> 00:12:59.920
as sort of a check to make
sure that what's been delivered

00:12:59.920 --> 00:13:02.800
was actually what
the specs said,

00:13:02.800 --> 00:13:04.300
and ultimately it means you do--

00:13:04.300 --> 00:13:05.264
AUDIENCE: Less work.

00:13:05.264 --> 00:13:06.347
MARSH GARDINER: Very good.

00:13:09.004 --> 00:13:10.420
I think gets really
interesting is

00:13:10.420 --> 00:13:13.820
when you use the spec as code
as the runtime's contract.

00:13:13.820 --> 00:13:17.260
So I mentioned a slide
or two ago a project

00:13:17.260 --> 00:13:18.100
called Swagger Node.

00:13:18.100 --> 00:13:23.020
We started something at Apigee,
a project called Apigee-127.

00:13:23.020 --> 00:13:24.464
It was pretty interesting.

00:13:24.464 --> 00:13:26.380
What it did is you could
define the interface.

00:13:26.380 --> 00:13:29.920
You'd design your API
interface, and then you

00:13:29.920 --> 00:13:32.372
would have a loose mapping
with the controllers that

00:13:32.372 --> 00:13:33.150
were the logic.

00:13:33.150 --> 00:13:35.142
So the problem when
you generate code,

00:13:35.142 --> 00:13:37.600
you're usually getting stubs,
then you add a bunch of logic

00:13:37.600 --> 00:13:37.900
into it.

00:13:37.900 --> 00:13:39.400
If you regenerate
the code, yoyou've

00:13:39.400 --> 00:13:41.290
got to figure a way
not to clobber that.

00:13:41.290 --> 00:13:42.400
There are ways to do that.

00:13:42.400 --> 00:13:43.160
Perfectly good.

00:13:43.160 --> 00:13:45.280
That's a totally valid workflow.

00:13:45.280 --> 00:13:47.320
But we wanted to
play with the idea

00:13:47.320 --> 00:13:51.640
that if you could use
the interface description

00:13:51.640 --> 00:13:53.140
as a definition--

00:13:53.140 --> 00:13:55.180
so the difference
being what is truth

00:13:55.180 --> 00:13:56.980
versus what might be truth--

00:13:56.980 --> 00:13:59.000
so if you're using it not
just as a description,

00:13:59.000 --> 00:14:00.780
but as the definition
of the services,

00:14:00.780 --> 00:14:02.440
and if you've got
a nice coupling

00:14:02.440 --> 00:14:05.030
to the logic and
the controllers,

00:14:05.030 --> 00:14:08.140
then you can really iterate
quickly on your design.

00:14:08.140 --> 00:14:11.065
You've got, for instance,
with Swagger Node--

00:14:11.065 --> 00:14:12.190
I didn't explain that part.

00:14:12.190 --> 00:14:15.490
Apigee-127 became Swagger Node,
was contributed to the Swagger

00:14:15.490 --> 00:14:21.480
universe as an official project,
and has the test generation

00:14:21.480 --> 00:14:24.361
piece in there, has
mocks in it as well.

00:14:24.361 --> 00:14:25.860
It's a pretty fun
way to build APIs.

00:14:25.860 --> 00:14:28.242
In fact, most of the APIs
that I have coded-- they

00:14:28.242 --> 00:14:29.700
don't let me code
very often, which

00:14:29.700 --> 00:14:30.991
is probably good for everybody.

00:14:30.991 --> 00:14:32.970
But most of the APIs
that I do write,

00:14:32.970 --> 00:14:36.850
I've done in Swagger
Node and Apigee-127,

00:14:36.850 --> 00:14:41.790
and I tend to deploy them to
Apigee because that's my job.

00:14:44.887 --> 00:14:46.470
Actually, there are
two other projects

00:14:46.470 --> 00:14:48.410
I should talk
about, though, here.

00:14:48.410 --> 00:14:51.580
There's a Python-based
approach--

00:14:51.580 --> 00:14:56.550
Python Flask-- called Connection
that also uses this approach.

00:14:56.550 --> 00:15:00.480
And then another Swagger product
called Swagger Inflector,

00:15:00.480 --> 00:15:07.020
that also has this interesting
coupling between the interface

00:15:07.020 --> 00:15:07.986
and the controllers.

00:15:12.426 --> 00:15:14.300
We've used each of those
things along the way

00:15:14.300 --> 00:15:17.105
to make our lives better
and do less work at Apigee,

00:15:17.105 --> 00:15:18.980
but what's been really
fun and interesting to

00:15:18.980 --> 00:15:22.130
me is to have been at Google
for the last three months.

00:15:22.130 --> 00:15:25.820
I think I'm still a
Noogler, technically.

00:15:25.820 --> 00:15:27.950
And it's been fascinating
to see Google's approach.

00:15:27.950 --> 00:15:30.680
And so even though I didn't
plan it for this talk

00:15:30.680 --> 00:15:33.362
when I proposed the
abstract a while ago,

00:15:33.362 --> 00:15:34.820
we're going to take
a little detour

00:15:34.820 --> 00:15:38.144
and talk about how Google
does APIs, because there are

00:15:38.144 --> 00:15:39.560
some interesting
things coming out

00:15:39.560 --> 00:15:43.070
of that too that I think are
worth at least talking about.

00:15:43.070 --> 00:15:46.040
So Google publishes
a lot of apps.

00:15:46.040 --> 00:15:50.102
They've got over 80
on the iOS app store.

00:15:50.102 --> 00:15:51.560
I don't know the
number on Android.

00:15:51.560 --> 00:15:53.860
I expect it's bigger.

00:15:53.860 --> 00:15:56.360
And they've got a lot of APIs--

00:15:56.360 --> 00:15:59.424
so many APIs, in fact, that they
need 14 different categories

00:15:59.424 --> 00:16:01.340
in order to be able to
present them to people.

00:16:01.340 --> 00:16:02.750
That's a lot of APIs.

00:16:02.750 --> 00:16:08.180
And the other
thing that has just

00:16:08.180 --> 00:16:11.030
blown my mind about learning
about how Google does things

00:16:11.030 --> 00:16:14.670
is their sense of scale.

00:16:14.670 --> 00:16:19.054
So this is a data center that's
in Mayes County, Oklahoma.

00:16:19.054 --> 00:16:20.720
We're going to have
a little data center

00:16:20.720 --> 00:16:21.729
porn for a few minutes.

00:16:21.729 --> 00:16:22.520
Indulge me, please.

00:16:22.520 --> 00:16:24.200
It's kind of fun and cool.

00:16:24.200 --> 00:16:26.660
This one's in North Carolina.

00:16:26.660 --> 00:16:28.740
This one's in Belgium.

00:16:28.740 --> 00:16:31.224
And I've started to think about
this as Google builds data

00:16:31.224 --> 00:16:32.390
center so you don't have to.

00:16:35.050 --> 00:16:36.700
I think you can
make a fun joke here

00:16:36.700 --> 00:16:40.620
about how the internet
is just a set of pipes.

00:16:40.620 --> 00:16:43.540
Well, these pipes-- they
actually carry water

00:16:43.540 --> 00:16:45.440
to cool the data center down.

00:16:45.440 --> 00:16:48.340
And for a sense of scale to
understand how big this is,

00:16:48.340 --> 00:16:49.449
so that's a Google bike.

00:16:49.449 --> 00:16:51.490
That's actually how we
get the data from one data

00:16:51.490 --> 00:16:52.240
center to another.

00:16:52.240 --> 00:16:54.830
We put them on
bikes and ride it.

00:16:54.830 --> 00:16:57.580
Just kidding.

00:16:57.580 --> 00:17:00.830
We have a lot of computers.

00:17:00.830 --> 00:17:03.220
And if you think about
it, if you try to add up

00:17:03.220 --> 00:17:05.349
all of the API
requests that happen

00:17:05.349 --> 00:17:07.030
in Google's infrastructure--

00:17:07.030 --> 00:17:09.609
that's every search query,
that's favoring a YouTube

00:17:09.609 --> 00:17:14.920
video, it's archiving
a Gmail, it's

00:17:14.920 --> 00:17:16.880
when you make a
wrong turn in Maps,

00:17:16.880 --> 00:17:18.130
it's your route recalculation.

00:17:18.130 --> 00:17:20.213
There are probably a bunch
of APIs calls in there.

00:17:20.213 --> 00:17:22.390
And some of those are
just a single API call

00:17:22.390 --> 00:17:23.560
to Google's infrastructure.

00:17:23.560 --> 00:17:26.569
That generally results in a ton
of calls in the back ends, too.

00:17:26.569 --> 00:17:30.400
So if you start to do some
back-of-the-envelope math,

00:17:30.400 --> 00:17:31.480
it adds up fast.

00:17:31.480 --> 00:17:33.688
Because all these computers
are talking to each other

00:17:33.688 --> 00:17:35.030
with APIs.

00:17:35.030 --> 00:17:39.420
And what that means is that
the systems that Google has,

00:17:39.420 --> 00:17:43.960
they can handle over 10
billion API calls every second.

00:17:43.960 --> 00:17:46.420
And I remember five years
ago when John Musser

00:17:46.420 --> 00:17:48.490
was talking about the
billionaire's club--

00:17:48.490 --> 00:17:53.110
like providers who were serving
a billion API calls in a month.

00:17:53.110 --> 00:17:56.090
And then multiply that by
10, and do that every second.

00:17:56.090 --> 00:17:57.580
And that boggles the mind.

00:17:57.580 --> 00:17:59.710
It's just so big
you can't really

00:17:59.710 --> 00:18:00.900
wrap your head around it.

00:18:00.900 --> 00:18:02.650
And that turns out to
be really important,

00:18:02.650 --> 00:18:04.630
and I'll come back
to that in a second.

00:18:04.630 --> 00:18:07.684
So Google, I'm going to
say about six years ago,

00:18:07.684 --> 00:18:08.350
seven years ago.

00:18:08.350 --> 00:18:10.450
Oh, let's do the math.

00:18:10.450 --> 00:18:13.480
OK, seven years ago--

00:18:13.480 --> 00:18:15.480
started doing its
own discovery format,

00:18:15.480 --> 00:18:17.177
it's a JSON-based format.

00:18:17.177 --> 00:18:18.760
This was happening
about the same time

00:18:18.760 --> 00:18:21.720
that Swagger was
coming out of Wordnik.

00:18:21.720 --> 00:18:26.976
And this was able to
drive a bunch of tooling,

00:18:26.976 --> 00:18:29.350
and they did this, they had
to roll their own description

00:18:29.350 --> 00:18:30.580
format at the time.

00:18:30.580 --> 00:18:31.540
A lot of you have
probably never heard

00:18:31.540 --> 00:18:32.956
about Google's
description format,

00:18:32.956 --> 00:18:36.414
because it was really to
solve a Google problem.

00:18:36.414 --> 00:18:38.080
So they needed it--
and I'll explain why

00:18:38.080 --> 00:18:39.746
in a second-- and
there was no standard,

00:18:39.746 --> 00:18:42.340
so they didn't have much choice.

00:18:42.340 --> 00:18:45.400
So this discovery document--

00:18:45.400 --> 00:18:48.092
there was a lot of
infrastructure based on it.

00:18:48.092 --> 00:18:49.550
They used it for
client generation.

00:18:49.550 --> 00:18:53.110
A lot of the reasons why I
talked about with API specs

00:18:53.110 --> 00:18:55.692
and why you want them, they
were taking advantage of that.

00:18:55.692 --> 00:18:57.692
So their discovery service
drove their explorer,

00:18:57.692 --> 00:19:00.010
it generated documentation.

00:19:00.010 --> 00:19:02.050
There were things
you couldn't see.

00:19:02.050 --> 00:19:05.680
They were able to take
the JSON-based APIs

00:19:05.680 --> 00:19:07.990
and map them into their
own internal format.

00:19:07.990 --> 00:19:10.540
And that starts to
get real interesting

00:19:10.540 --> 00:19:12.420
when we talk about scale.

00:19:12.420 --> 00:19:14.740
That worked-- I mean,
it solved their problem,

00:19:14.740 --> 00:19:16.690
but it didn't solve
other people's problems.

00:19:16.690 --> 00:19:20.080
They weren't interested in
open-sourcing it or formally

00:19:20.080 --> 00:19:22.090
making that a format
for other people to use.

00:19:22.090 --> 00:19:24.042
It was really to
scratch their own itch.

00:19:24.042 --> 00:19:26.500
And that meant that there was
no open-source community that

00:19:26.500 --> 00:19:29.370
sprang up around it.

00:19:29.370 --> 00:19:34.400
And so lots of things happening,
everything's evolving.

00:19:34.400 --> 00:19:37.040
And people realized it.

00:19:37.040 --> 00:19:38.540
So there have been
people doing what

00:19:38.540 --> 00:19:44.150
are called protobufs at Google
for at least a dozen years,

00:19:44.150 --> 00:19:45.500
I think.

00:19:45.500 --> 00:19:47.600
And that's how Google sends
things back and forth,

00:19:47.600 --> 00:19:50.630
and that's how they
describe their data.

00:19:50.630 --> 00:19:53.750
And it takes advantage of things
like HTTP/2, bi-directional

00:19:53.750 --> 00:19:54.710
streaming.

00:19:54.710 --> 00:19:58.144
And so Google was using
all this internally,

00:19:58.144 --> 00:20:00.560
and the lesson they learned
from doing their own discovery

00:20:00.560 --> 00:20:03.470
format, in part, was
that if they really

00:20:03.470 --> 00:20:07.910
wanted the rest of the
world to appreciate and do

00:20:07.910 --> 00:20:10.310
things the Google-y
way, then they

00:20:10.310 --> 00:20:12.440
should do their next
version-- so that service was

00:20:12.440 --> 00:20:13.250
called Stubby.

00:20:13.250 --> 00:20:14.999
They should do the
next version of Stubby.

00:20:14.999 --> 00:20:15.800
It's called gRPC.

00:20:15.800 --> 00:20:18.274
And they should do
that in the open.

00:20:18.274 --> 00:20:21.650
So this was a
little weird to me.

00:20:21.650 --> 00:20:26.447
I get it, it makes a lot of
sense, but it's taken me--

00:20:26.447 --> 00:20:28.780
I would say it's three months
to even sort of understand

00:20:28.780 --> 00:20:31.224
how these things
start to fit together.

00:20:31.224 --> 00:20:32.890
Protocol buffers are
pretty interesting.

00:20:32.890 --> 00:20:35.400
They're language neutral,
platform neutral.

00:20:35.400 --> 00:20:37.510
They're extensible.

00:20:37.510 --> 00:20:41.330
I like to think of them
as sort of three things.

00:20:41.330 --> 00:20:44.020
One, they're a
serialization mechanism.

00:20:44.020 --> 00:20:46.600
Two, they are a kind of
interface description language.

00:20:46.600 --> 00:20:48.430
And three, they're
sort of a methodology.

00:20:48.430 --> 00:20:50.470
And that methodology is going
to sound kind of familiar,

00:20:50.470 --> 00:20:51.790
because we were just
talking about that

00:20:51.790 --> 00:20:53.687
when I was explaining
OpenAPI specs at Apigee

00:20:53.687 --> 00:20:54.520
and how we use them.

00:20:57.600 --> 00:20:59.440
Protocol buffers are a
bit like JSON schema.

00:20:59.440 --> 00:21:01.940
You use them to describe
the structure of your data.

00:21:01.940 --> 00:21:04.234
gRPC gives you this framework.

00:21:04.234 --> 00:21:06.400
You get a bunch of tooling
that comes along with it.

00:21:06.400 --> 00:21:09.130
And this is what
Google uses internally

00:21:09.130 --> 00:21:12.060
to do that massive
number of APIs

00:21:12.060 --> 00:21:13.787
that I was talking
about earlier.

00:21:13.787 --> 00:21:15.370
What this all means,
if you add it up,

00:21:15.370 --> 00:21:18.340
is that if Google used
OpenAPI instead of gRPC,

00:21:18.340 --> 00:21:20.060
we'd probably need
another data center.

00:21:20.060 --> 00:21:21.851
And that's hundreds of
millions of dollars.

00:21:21.851 --> 00:21:23.700
That's an expensive thing.

00:21:23.700 --> 00:21:26.076
Because when we have to send
all of that information back

00:21:26.076 --> 00:21:27.575
and forth across
all those computers

00:21:27.575 --> 00:21:29.140
that we saw those
pictures of before,

00:21:29.140 --> 00:21:35.050
it actually makes sense to go
to a binary format over the wire

00:21:35.050 --> 00:21:37.090
just for efficiency--

00:21:37.090 --> 00:21:40.150
not everybody has that problem.

00:21:40.150 --> 00:21:43.810
Others have started
to adopt gRPC too.

00:21:43.810 --> 00:21:45.010
Netflix talked about this.

00:21:45.010 --> 00:21:47.680
They took one of
their main projects,

00:21:47.680 --> 00:21:49.470
Ribbon, I think it
was, and instead

00:21:49.470 --> 00:21:54.100
they're now going to be building
their solution on top of gRPC.

00:21:54.100 --> 00:21:56.230
Also interesting and pertinent--

00:21:56.230 --> 00:22:00.070
March 1, the Cloud Native
Computing Foundation

00:22:00.070 --> 00:22:02.920
became the parent of gRPC.

00:22:02.920 --> 00:22:06.010
This wasn't meant to
be a Google thing.

00:22:06.010 --> 00:22:09.100
This is meant to
belong somewhere else.

00:22:09.100 --> 00:22:12.436
And so it's neat and interesting
that it's in the Cloud Native

00:22:12.436 --> 00:22:13.810
Computing Foundation
for a reason

00:22:13.810 --> 00:22:17.170
we'll come to in a second.

00:22:17.170 --> 00:22:18.570
Why two, though?

00:22:18.570 --> 00:22:21.820
Because Google's involved
in both gRPC and in OpenAPI.

00:22:23.961 --> 00:22:25.960
To synthesize some of the
things we were talking

00:22:25.960 --> 00:22:27.970
about in the beginning,
you get things

00:22:27.970 --> 00:22:30.730
like documentation, your code
generation, your API service

00:22:30.730 --> 00:22:32.980
configuration--

00:22:32.980 --> 00:22:36.400
all of those benefits of
embracing the way of the spec.

00:22:36.400 --> 00:22:41.170
So we see the industry moving in
a direction that fits with how

00:22:41.170 --> 00:22:43.765
we think things work best.

00:22:43.765 --> 00:22:45.700
It's so funny to
say "we," actually.

00:22:45.700 --> 00:22:47.059
[INAUDIBLE]

00:22:47.059 --> 00:22:49.600
But it's also really the thing
that both are Linux Foundation

00:22:49.600 --> 00:22:51.884
projects.

00:22:51.884 --> 00:22:53.800
We think The Linux
Foundation is pretty great,

00:22:53.800 --> 00:22:56.430
and it makes a great home
for both of these things.

00:22:56.430 --> 00:22:58.720
And we are interested in
figuring out over time

00:22:58.720 --> 00:23:02.410
how we can bring
those things together.

00:23:02.410 --> 00:23:04.190
That may be off in
the distance a bit.

00:23:04.190 --> 00:23:08.480
We've been trying to figure
out how and to help--

00:23:08.480 --> 00:23:10.730
at least with the 3.0
evolution of the spec--

00:23:10.730 --> 00:23:13.190
consider ways in which these
things could start to come

00:23:13.190 --> 00:23:16.475
together over time, but
right now they aren't.

00:23:19.340 --> 00:23:21.290
Google uses OpenAPI
specs in other ways

00:23:21.290 --> 00:23:23.540
because there are some nice
adoption benefits from it.

00:23:26.870 --> 00:23:30.330
The new one platform API--

00:23:30.330 --> 00:23:32.340
sorry, GCPs.

00:23:32.340 --> 00:23:36.690
The way we manage
APIs internally--

00:23:36.690 --> 00:23:38.660
we used to serve up
that description format

00:23:38.660 --> 00:23:40.826
that I talked about, that
they created on their own.

00:23:40.826 --> 00:23:43.710
Now you can actually
get an OpenAPI format

00:23:43.710 --> 00:23:47.250
of that description instead.

00:23:47.250 --> 00:23:51.644
Kubernetes publishes--
people know Kubernetes.

00:23:51.644 --> 00:23:54.060
But just in case you don't,
this will explain a little bit

00:23:54.060 --> 00:23:54.990
about what Kubernetes is.

00:23:54.990 --> 00:23:55.990
I'm no expert in this.

00:23:55.990 --> 00:23:57.660
But the thing that
is interesting

00:23:57.660 --> 00:24:04.770
is that Kubernetes publishes its
OpenAPI specs with a Kubernetes

00:24:04.770 --> 00:24:06.690
server.

00:24:06.690 --> 00:24:09.470
At Apigee, we use the OpenAPI--
because we're part of Google

00:24:09.470 --> 00:24:11.970
now I can say this is how Google
[? uses specs. As ?] Apigee

00:24:11.970 --> 00:24:13.620
uses specs, we use it
at different points

00:24:13.620 --> 00:24:14.328
in the lifecycle.

00:24:14.328 --> 00:24:16.020
We've got a spec editor.

00:24:16.020 --> 00:24:18.950
You can generate proxies
and flows from specs.

00:24:18.950 --> 00:24:22.370
And API documentation
is generated from specs.

00:24:22.370 --> 00:24:24.750
Cloud Endpoints--
it's an API gateway,

00:24:24.750 --> 00:24:27.400
and its configuration can be
done with an OpenAPI spec,

00:24:27.400 --> 00:24:29.620
so you can start to
see that OpenAPIs--

00:24:29.620 --> 00:24:31.560
that we expect the
rest of the world

00:24:31.560 --> 00:24:34.437
to be speaking OpenAPI
and not gRPC, necessarily.

00:24:34.437 --> 00:24:36.270
So we've had to live
in both of these worlds

00:24:36.270 --> 00:24:40.710
and figure out how to live in
both of them at the same time.

00:24:40.710 --> 00:24:44.540
So that's a bit
about how we deal

00:24:44.540 --> 00:24:47.420
with specs at Apigee
and Google, in general,

00:24:47.420 --> 00:24:50.120
and in the world at large.

00:24:50.120 --> 00:24:53.130
My advice would be, if you think
scale is your biggest problem,

00:24:53.130 --> 00:24:55.010
gRPC might be a
good fit for you.

00:24:55.010 --> 00:24:57.690
It certainly works well for us.

00:24:57.690 --> 00:25:01.414
And if you're concerned more
about adoption, OpenAPI.

00:25:01.414 --> 00:25:03.580
And there may be some cases
where you consider both.

00:25:03.580 --> 00:25:05.550
That's where we are, actually.

00:25:05.550 --> 00:25:07.497
And the reason why
I said option one,

00:25:07.497 --> 00:25:09.080
there's just more
attraction out there

00:25:09.080 --> 00:25:10.630
in the world with OpenAPI.

00:25:10.630 --> 00:25:17.030
Two, it has a benefit of being
a text-based description format,

00:25:17.030 --> 00:25:20.640
and generally speaking, it
describes text-based responses

00:25:20.640 --> 00:25:22.190
or serializations.

00:25:22.190 --> 00:25:24.620
So just like when you
see a web page you

00:25:24.620 --> 00:25:28.310
can view source and see the HTML
and the JavaScript and the CSS,

00:25:28.310 --> 00:25:30.050
you can see what's
actually being sent

00:25:30.050 --> 00:25:31.590
back and forth across the wire.

00:25:31.590 --> 00:25:33.090
That is just much
more approachable.

00:25:33.090 --> 00:25:36.160
You get more visibility
into how the system works.

00:25:36.160 --> 00:25:37.430
There's less mystery.

00:25:37.430 --> 00:25:40.124
Whereas if you're
dealing with binary,

00:25:40.124 --> 00:25:42.290
unless you've got some
special tooling to figure out

00:25:42.290 --> 00:25:44.123
how to unpack that,
you're asking developers

00:25:44.123 --> 00:25:46.620
to make a big leap
to understand why.

00:25:46.620 --> 00:25:49.389
And so I tend to think of
this as an adoption benefit.

00:25:49.389 --> 00:25:50.930
Because what I see
with our customers

00:25:50.930 --> 00:25:52.280
all the time is that
they're trying to figure out

00:25:52.280 --> 00:25:53.780
how do they deliver
value to people,

00:25:53.780 --> 00:25:57.290
and how do their developers
realize that value quickly,

00:25:57.290 --> 00:25:59.510
and if the format's
getting in the way, that

00:25:59.510 --> 00:26:01.289
could be a problem.

00:26:01.289 --> 00:26:03.330
So I don't think it's an
easy question to answer.

00:26:03.330 --> 00:26:05.870
I think there's a lot
of interesting stuff

00:26:05.870 --> 00:26:08.360
to chew into, particularly
now that both projects are

00:26:08.360 --> 00:26:11.190
in The Linux Foundation,
we'll see how that goes.

00:26:11.190 --> 00:26:13.070
But the takeaway is use specs.

00:26:16.772 --> 00:26:17.980
Good, I was a little worried.

00:26:17.980 --> 00:26:20.930
It's a lot of content, and
we're making good time.

00:26:20.930 --> 00:26:22.820
This is where we start
to get pretty nerdy,

00:26:22.820 --> 00:26:26.450
so if you want to understand
how the evolution of 2.0

00:26:26.450 --> 00:26:30.470
is getting to 3.0,
there's a lot here,

00:26:30.470 --> 00:26:34.100
and I'll recommend that all of
this is happening in GitHub.

00:26:34.100 --> 00:26:36.950
And so you can get involved--

00:26:36.950 --> 00:26:38.705
file issues, ask questions.

00:26:43.000 --> 00:26:45.340
When we hit the implementer's
milestone last week,

00:26:45.340 --> 00:26:48.640
really this means that there
are very few changes to how

00:26:48.640 --> 00:26:50.440
the spec works from
this point forward,

00:26:50.440 --> 00:26:52.930
definitely the focus
is on documentation.

00:26:52.930 --> 00:26:55.360
But we're also at the point
that the challenge we have

00:26:55.360 --> 00:26:57.235
is that there's this
chicken and egg problem.

00:26:57.235 --> 00:26:59.443
People don't care about the
spec without the tooling,

00:26:59.443 --> 00:27:01.720
and the tooling can't
spring up around the spec

00:27:01.720 --> 00:27:04.012
until it hardens enough to
be able to build against it,

00:27:04.012 --> 00:27:05.678
and that's the point
where we're at now.

00:27:05.678 --> 00:27:07.210
So now it's stable
enough that you

00:27:07.210 --> 00:27:09.510
can begin to build against it.

00:27:09.510 --> 00:27:11.260
And we'll see.

00:27:11.260 --> 00:27:14.400
I'm not sure how long it
will take for things to flip

00:27:14.400 --> 00:27:19.140
and for 2.0 to become the
old, out of fashion version

00:27:19.140 --> 00:27:20.390
and 3.0 to pick up.

00:27:20.390 --> 00:27:22.431
But it's going to happen
sometime in the next six

00:27:22.431 --> 00:27:24.560
to 12 months is my
guess, but first we

00:27:24.560 --> 00:27:26.800
go through the
Implementer's Draft phase.

00:27:26.800 --> 00:27:28.900
We'll cover a bunch of
the big changes today.

00:27:28.900 --> 00:27:32.020
If you remember one thing, the
easiest place to get back to

00:27:32.020 --> 00:27:34.582
is the Open API
Initiative's blog.

00:27:34.582 --> 00:27:36.040
We've been talking
about and trying

00:27:36.040 --> 00:27:39.270
to help the community
understand what's changing.

00:27:39.270 --> 00:27:41.020
We have pictures of
unicorns and rainbows,

00:27:41.020 --> 00:27:43.958
so it's got to be good.

00:27:43.958 --> 00:27:46.710
That's how we get the
younger demographic in too.

00:27:49.630 --> 00:27:52.265
A lot of this work has
happened with the TDC.

00:27:52.265 --> 00:27:54.980
So that's the Technical
Developer Community.

00:27:54.980 --> 00:27:56.890
That's me-- I'm
trapped in an iPhone.

00:27:56.890 --> 00:27:58.150
And Tony Tam--

00:28:00.664 --> 00:28:02.830
Swagger's his baby, he's
been there for a long time.

00:28:02.830 --> 00:28:07.090
Ron [? Rotoski-- ?] both
those folks were at Wordnik

00:28:07.090 --> 00:28:09.760
and are now at SmartBear.

00:28:09.760 --> 00:28:12.754
And Ron probably knows the
spec as well as anybody,

00:28:12.754 --> 00:28:13.920
like forwards and backwards.

00:28:13.920 --> 00:28:15.902
He's where I go when
I have questions, too.

00:28:15.902 --> 00:28:17.110
Darrel Miller from Microsoft.

00:28:17.110 --> 00:28:17.776
He's been great.

00:28:17.776 --> 00:28:20.950
And Jason Harmon, who
had been at PayPal

00:28:20.950 --> 00:28:24.080
when he started working on the
TDC and is now at Typeform.

00:28:26.920 --> 00:28:29.020
I spent a lot of quality
time on conference calls

00:28:29.020 --> 00:28:31.986
with these folks.

00:28:31.986 --> 00:28:34.360
The Implementer's Draft-- it
represents about a year work

00:28:34.360 --> 00:28:37.010
by the TDC and the community,
because it's not just the TDC

00:28:37.010 --> 00:28:38.900
saying, it must be like this.

00:28:38.900 --> 00:28:41.080
It's people filing issues
and us figuring out

00:28:41.080 --> 00:28:44.080
how to reconcile that with what
the spec should and shouldn't

00:28:44.080 --> 00:28:45.370
do.

00:28:45.370 --> 00:28:47.530
It means that the third
generation of the spec

00:28:47.530 --> 00:28:50.334
is now relatively stable.

00:28:50.334 --> 00:28:52.750
And as I was saying, we have
this chicken and egg problem.

00:28:52.750 --> 00:28:54.791
The spec has got to be
stable enough for tooling,

00:28:54.791 --> 00:28:58.990
and that's going to start to
become cleaner and tighter.

00:28:58.990 --> 00:29:00.790
And at this point,
only blocking issues.

00:29:00.790 --> 00:29:04.030
Like if we discover we made
some terrible oversight, that

00:29:04.030 --> 00:29:05.275
would get fixed.

00:29:05.275 --> 00:29:07.150
But most of this is
going to be documentation

00:29:07.150 --> 00:29:09.640
and giving people the
supporting information

00:29:09.640 --> 00:29:11.627
they need to be successful.

00:29:11.627 --> 00:29:13.210
And this has been
happening-- at least

00:29:13.210 --> 00:29:15.354
until we hit the full
release of the [INAUDIBLE].

00:29:15.354 --> 00:29:17.770
It's still happening in a
branch they called OpenAPI.next.

00:29:17.770 --> 00:29:21.162
So if you arrive at the GitHub
repo and you see Version 2,

00:29:21.162 --> 00:29:23.120
that's because it's still
the official version.

00:29:23.120 --> 00:29:26.910
It is the only released
version of the OpenAPI spec.

00:29:26.910 --> 00:29:31.150
And so 3.0 will replace
that eventually.

00:29:31.150 --> 00:29:33.860
This is a great time to
start getting involved,

00:29:33.860 --> 00:29:36.342
particularly if you
are doing tooling.

00:29:36.342 --> 00:29:38.800
You can get a lot of attention
from people in the developer

00:29:38.800 --> 00:29:39.970
community now.

00:29:39.970 --> 00:29:41.850
So if you are thinking
about getting involved

00:29:41.850 --> 00:29:44.410
in creating tooling on
top OpenAPI, particularly

00:29:44.410 --> 00:29:46.570
open source, this might
be a great time to do it.

00:29:46.570 --> 00:29:52.570
It will also really help the
spec become a real thing.

00:29:52.570 --> 00:29:57.069
So I think this is the
simplest way to understand

00:29:57.069 --> 00:29:58.360
what's happened between 2 to 3.

00:30:02.861 --> 00:30:04.360
This is just a
visual representation

00:30:04.360 --> 00:30:06.850
of the major high-level
sections of the spec.

00:30:06.850 --> 00:30:09.070
And it's a lot cleaner
and simpler on the right.

00:30:09.070 --> 00:30:11.200
Things like components.

00:30:11.200 --> 00:30:13.450
So we took a bunch of these
reusable definitions that

00:30:13.450 --> 00:30:14.590
were sort of
scattered throughout,

00:30:14.590 --> 00:30:16.423
and we brought them
together under one thing

00:30:16.423 --> 00:30:18.670
called "Components."

00:30:18.670 --> 00:30:21.214
Obviously, the spec is now 3.0.

00:30:21.214 --> 00:30:21.880
That's exciting.

00:30:21.880 --> 00:30:23.456
But it's not just that.

00:30:23.456 --> 00:30:25.580
We have introduced Semantic
Versioning as well with

00:30:25.580 --> 00:30:28.420
the specs, whereas
before it was just 2.0.

00:30:28.420 --> 00:30:33.560
Now we're trying to follow a
Semantic Versioning approach.

00:30:33.560 --> 00:30:35.050
So clean up of the top level.

00:30:35.050 --> 00:30:37.660
And in general-- and I'll
try to point this out as

00:30:37.660 --> 00:30:38.990
we go through some of these.

00:30:38.990 --> 00:30:40.990
We reconsidered some of
the names along the way.

00:30:40.990 --> 00:30:42.790
So Host is a good
example, and we'll

00:30:42.790 --> 00:30:44.920
talk about that in a second.

00:30:44.920 --> 00:30:49.720
We now use Host to
Security instead.

00:30:49.720 --> 00:30:52.129
I think that's all the
stuff I have on this one.

00:30:52.129 --> 00:30:54.170
We're going to start with
some of the easy things

00:30:54.170 --> 00:30:55.360
to warm up with.

00:30:55.360 --> 00:31:00.290
It used to be you could
use description fields,

00:31:00.290 --> 00:31:04.760
you could give mark down,
and we had, in the 2.0 specs,

00:31:04.760 --> 00:31:06.336
said "GitHub Flavored."

00:31:06.336 --> 00:31:07.460
It just sort of made sense.

00:31:07.460 --> 00:31:09.422
It was on GitHub and it
was easy to deal with.

00:31:09.422 --> 00:31:11.630
But the GitHub-Flavored
mark-down didn't have a spec,

00:31:11.630 --> 00:31:14.750
and so Apiary and
API Blueprint also

00:31:14.750 --> 00:31:19.150
moved from GitHub-Flavored
mark-down to common mark,

00:31:19.150 --> 00:31:22.460
because there's a spec.

00:31:22.460 --> 00:31:26.040
Also, I think leveraging
other specs where

00:31:26.040 --> 00:31:31.274
we can, like URI templates,
you will see this

00:31:31.274 --> 00:31:32.440
come up over and over again.

00:31:32.440 --> 00:31:33.564
I think we did a great job.

00:31:33.564 --> 00:31:35.490
Darrel's been particularly
good at bringing

00:31:35.490 --> 00:31:36.906
specs that exist
in the real world

00:31:36.906 --> 00:31:39.440
so that we don't
reinvent things.

00:31:39.440 --> 00:31:42.450
Another small change is
that we moved from YAML 1.1

00:31:42.450 --> 00:31:47.530
to YAML 1.2.

00:31:47.530 --> 00:31:50.960
In the info object, there
is a breaking change.

00:31:50.960 --> 00:31:54.980
The terms of service
is now a URL.

00:31:54.980 --> 00:31:58.610
And something that often
catches people by surprise--

00:31:58.610 --> 00:32:03.530
this version here is not
the version of the spec,

00:32:03.530 --> 00:32:05.990
or it's not the spec
document itself.

00:32:05.990 --> 00:32:07.080
This describes the API.

00:32:07.080 --> 00:32:08.330
That's the version of the API.

00:32:12.600 --> 00:32:14.280
This came up a lot.

00:32:14.280 --> 00:32:16.080
Multiple servers.

00:32:16.080 --> 00:32:18.960
So now you can actually declare
a bunch of different servers.

00:32:18.960 --> 00:32:21.780
This can be a little tricky
because sometimes if you're

00:32:21.780 --> 00:32:23.460
dealing with a
staging environment,

00:32:23.460 --> 00:32:26.160
it may be that it's not
the same specification.

00:32:26.160 --> 00:32:27.700
You're introducing a new API.

00:32:27.700 --> 00:32:30.030
And so I would say be a
little bit careful here.

00:32:30.030 --> 00:32:31.590
But if you have
a situation where

00:32:31.590 --> 00:32:33.360
you were running
multiple servers,

00:32:33.360 --> 00:32:35.040
then this starts to make sense.

00:32:35.040 --> 00:32:38.880
It makes even more sense when
you add in parameterization.

00:32:38.880 --> 00:32:42.817
So now, for instance,
if your host name

00:32:42.817 --> 00:32:44.400
changes based on
some pattern, there's

00:32:44.400 --> 00:32:47.670
a template that lets you do
that and some variables you

00:32:47.670 --> 00:32:51.960
can leverage to enable that.

00:32:51.960 --> 00:32:54.030
One word of warning
here is that one

00:32:54.030 --> 00:32:57.050
of the trickiest things
of all in the 2.0 spec

00:32:57.050 --> 00:32:58.980
was how we used
the word "default."

00:32:58.980 --> 00:33:02.400
We use default to describe
parameter values--

00:33:02.400 --> 00:33:05.310
that if the client
didn't supply a parameter

00:33:05.310 --> 00:33:09.960
value, that's what the server
was going to substitute for it.

00:33:09.960 --> 00:33:13.090
That's what the server's
initialization would be.

00:33:13.090 --> 00:33:16.380
Here, however, if you
don't have a user name,

00:33:16.380 --> 00:33:18.480
this URL isn't going to resolve.

00:33:18.480 --> 00:33:21.082
So this isn't simply
what the server will do

00:33:21.082 --> 00:33:22.290
if you don't supply anything.

00:33:22.290 --> 00:33:26.965
In this case, a default means
something a tiny bit different.

00:33:26.965 --> 00:33:28.590
We probably talked
for 15 minutes about

00:33:28.590 --> 00:33:29.850
whether we should call
it something else.

00:33:29.850 --> 00:33:31.410
We couldn't come up
with a better name.

00:33:31.410 --> 00:33:32.326
These things are hard.

00:33:32.326 --> 00:33:35.460
And that sort of exemplifies
what these conversations--

00:33:35.460 --> 00:33:38.010
we can have an hour and a half
conversation about Webhooks

00:33:38.010 --> 00:33:39.718
and how whether or
not they deserve to be

00:33:39.718 --> 00:33:41.010
part of the specification.

00:33:41.010 --> 00:33:43.080
Some of these calls,
we recorded them all.

00:33:43.080 --> 00:33:45.952
Some of them are really boring,
talking about things like that.

00:33:45.952 --> 00:33:47.410
But, you know, fun
and passionate--

00:33:47.410 --> 00:33:49.110
a bunch of people who are
really interested in what's

00:33:49.110 --> 00:33:50.568
the right way to
describe the APIs.

00:33:52.940 --> 00:33:55.000
I mentioned the reusable
components before.

00:33:55.000 --> 00:33:57.480
So we collected a bunch of
things and added a few more.

00:33:57.480 --> 00:34:03.990
So one thing to remember is
that all of these components

00:34:03.990 --> 00:34:05.370
require some sort
of a reference.

00:34:05.370 --> 00:34:08.730
We use JSON pointers for that.

00:34:08.730 --> 00:34:11.010
We also introduced
in the components

00:34:11.010 --> 00:34:12.600
some restrictions
around the name.

00:34:12.600 --> 00:34:14.460
I think there was no character
restriction on the names

00:34:14.460 --> 00:34:16.210
before, that introduced
all kinds of bugs.

00:34:16.210 --> 00:34:20.310
People would come up and try to
put Unicode in their definition

00:34:20.310 --> 00:34:20.810
name.

00:34:20.810 --> 00:34:21.630
That's a bad idea.

00:34:21.630 --> 00:34:25.139
So we decided it's easier to be
restrictive early and expand it

00:34:25.139 --> 00:34:27.210
later if there is need.

00:34:27.210 --> 00:34:29.820
If you do want to
namespace your components

00:34:29.820 --> 00:34:31.860
we recommend using
a dot notation.

00:34:31.860 --> 00:34:35.679
That's one of the
accepted characters.

00:34:35.679 --> 00:34:39.850
Another thing that came up
surprisingly often with 2.0

00:34:39.850 --> 00:34:44.110
was why can't we add a
description to the resource,

00:34:44.110 --> 00:34:46.482
to the path?

00:34:46.482 --> 00:34:47.440
And, you know, why not?

00:34:47.440 --> 00:34:49.179
It's a good idea.

00:34:49.179 --> 00:34:49.840
It makes sense.

00:34:49.840 --> 00:34:51.820
It should belong there.

00:34:51.820 --> 00:34:54.489
Another thing you can do here
is you can override servers now,

00:34:54.489 --> 00:34:56.320
at the path level.

00:34:56.320 --> 00:34:58.032
And we introduced
support for Trace,

00:34:58.032 --> 00:35:00.490
because, hey, it's an [? HTV ?]
verb and we should do that.

00:35:03.420 --> 00:35:07.560
We made an effort to do a better
job with URI template spec

00:35:07.560 --> 00:35:11.510
and keeping that compatible.

00:35:11.510 --> 00:35:17.390
These schemas for parameters
are basically primitive types.

00:35:17.390 --> 00:35:20.711
One thing you'll notice is
that the form "body" isn't here

00:35:20.711 --> 00:35:21.210
anymore.

00:35:21.210 --> 00:35:24.264
It used to be that you would
have to say of a parameter--

00:35:24.264 --> 00:35:26.180
you'd have to give it's
location as "in body,"

00:35:26.180 --> 00:35:28.180
and you'll see how we can
deal with that better.

00:35:32.250 --> 00:35:34.740
So it requested content body.

00:35:34.740 --> 00:35:38.130
This is what replaced that.

00:35:38.130 --> 00:35:41.850
You can now describe form
data using schema definitions.

00:35:41.850 --> 00:35:43.130
That's kind of interesting.

00:35:43.130 --> 00:35:47.100
You'll notice the
content block here.

00:35:47.100 --> 00:35:49.150
And there are now media types.

00:35:49.150 --> 00:35:50.910
So you can describe
different media types,

00:35:50.910 --> 00:35:53.130
and they can have different
examples and different schemas.

00:35:53.130 --> 00:35:53.880
That's a big deal.

00:35:53.880 --> 00:36:00.400
We can only have, I think, one
example per operation in 2.0.

00:36:00.400 --> 00:36:01.930
I might not have
that exactly right.

00:36:01.930 --> 00:36:04.270
But now you can define
any number of media types.

00:36:04.270 --> 00:36:05.470
That's cool and interesting.

00:36:05.470 --> 00:36:09.250
It also ends up replacing,
produces, and consumes,

00:36:09.250 --> 00:36:11.950
which I had always
found a little tricky

00:36:11.950 --> 00:36:14.302
in how OpenAPI and
Swagger before it

00:36:14.302 --> 00:36:15.760
had tried to describe
these things.

00:36:15.760 --> 00:36:18.010
So I think it's a much
cleaner, nicer way of doing it.

00:36:20.470 --> 00:36:23.200
Similarly for responses, you've
got multiple content types.

00:36:23.200 --> 00:36:27.640
And you can now wildcard status
codes, which is kind of cool.

00:36:27.640 --> 00:36:29.200
And there was an
amusing conversation

00:36:29.200 --> 00:36:31.630
about how many Xs we should
allow in status codes.

00:36:34.467 --> 00:36:37.050
I should probably have written
down what video we did that in,

00:36:37.050 --> 00:36:37.966
because it was funny--

00:36:40.440 --> 00:36:41.285
what call that was.

00:36:41.285 --> 00:36:42.660
Another interesting
piece is that

00:36:42.660 --> 00:36:45.670
what is a callback in a Webhook
if not really just another kind

00:36:45.670 --> 00:36:46.170
of API?

00:36:46.170 --> 00:36:48.490
It's more like a push
API in that case.

00:36:48.490 --> 00:36:50.460
And one really
important piece of this

00:36:50.460 --> 00:36:54.120
is to be able to refer back
into what you're getting back.

00:36:54.120 --> 00:36:56.790
So you'll notice the
request body URL.

00:36:56.790 --> 00:37:00.072
That's something that you're
looking for in the response.

00:37:00.072 --> 00:37:01.030
And that's interesting.

00:37:01.030 --> 00:37:04.490
I definitely recommend looking
at the callbacks objects.

00:37:04.490 --> 00:37:07.650
Darrel Miller did a bunch
of work describing that.

00:37:07.650 --> 00:37:11.040
It's good if you're
struggling with also

00:37:11.040 --> 00:37:12.695
describing your Webhooks.

00:37:12.695 --> 00:37:14.070
This could be
interesting to you.

00:37:17.470 --> 00:37:19.210
We also tried to be
more accommodating

00:37:19.210 --> 00:37:23.140
of hypermedia-- not necessarily
as the engine of application

00:37:23.140 --> 00:37:26.680
state, but just realizing
that hypermedia is used a lot

00:37:26.680 --> 00:37:28.630
to point at different things.

00:37:28.630 --> 00:37:32.140
We wanted to be able to do a
better job supporting that.

00:37:32.140 --> 00:37:34.390
And so now it's possible to
use these links to connect

00:37:34.390 --> 00:37:38.740
operations from responses,
and that operation can either

00:37:38.740 --> 00:37:42.160
be an ID or it actually
can be a full href.

00:37:42.160 --> 00:37:44.320
And this, to some degree,
means that documentation

00:37:44.320 --> 00:37:46.850
is no longer flat,
because you start

00:37:46.850 --> 00:37:49.390
to be able to interconnect
these operations,

00:37:49.390 --> 00:37:52.760
and that's kind of
cool and interesting.

00:37:52.760 --> 00:37:55.090
So if that strikes
you, definitely

00:37:55.090 --> 00:37:57.824
check that in the
OpenAPI.next spec.

00:37:57.824 --> 00:37:59.740
We didn't change a lot
of security definitions

00:37:59.740 --> 00:38:02.920
but we did one thing that
was really important.

00:38:02.920 --> 00:38:07.720
The flows that we had in 2.0
didn't follow the the OAuth 2.0

00:38:07.720 --> 00:38:11.620
spec, and so as a general
hygiene and cleanup,

00:38:11.620 --> 00:38:12.940
we fixed that.

00:38:12.940 --> 00:38:15.280
So that's good.

00:38:15.280 --> 00:38:17.590
I don't know how we
missed that in 2.0,

00:38:17.590 --> 00:38:21.422
but I'm pleased to say
that that's right now.

00:38:21.422 --> 00:38:23.630
JSON schema is the thing
that gets everybody excited.

00:38:23.630 --> 00:38:26.120
I don't know why,
exactly, but I think

00:38:26.120 --> 00:38:29.030
it was in part because 2.0 had
such restrictions around what

00:38:29.030 --> 00:38:30.770
you were allowed to do when
you're describing things,

00:38:30.770 --> 00:38:32.210
and that didn't fit
a lot of the APIs

00:38:32.210 --> 00:38:33.751
that are out there
in the real world.

00:38:33.751 --> 00:38:35.460
So now you can use--

00:38:35.460 --> 00:38:37.310
and there are a
bunch of caveats.

00:38:37.310 --> 00:38:41.300
You should definitely read into
this carefully in the spec.

00:38:41.300 --> 00:38:44.210
But I am no expert
in JSON schema.

00:38:44.210 --> 00:38:46.470
I would recommend if
you are, check that out.

00:38:46.470 --> 00:38:49.460
But you are now able to use
allOf and oneOf and anyOf

00:38:49.460 --> 00:38:51.650
and some of the other
interesting pieces

00:38:51.650 --> 00:38:53.360
that JSON schema allows.

00:38:53.360 --> 00:38:55.100
It's not full JSON
schema support,

00:38:55.100 --> 00:38:57.452
but it's a whole lot
closer to it than it was.

00:38:57.452 --> 00:38:58.910
This is good, and
it's helpful also

00:38:58.910 --> 00:39:01.130
to take advantage of all
the great JSON tools that

00:39:01.130 --> 00:39:02.255
are out there in the world.

00:39:05.610 --> 00:39:07.700
In a quick summary--

00:39:07.700 --> 00:39:11.390
I want to make sure we have
enough time for questions--

00:39:11.390 --> 00:39:13.490
really what's happened
with the 3.0 spec is

00:39:13.490 --> 00:39:16.430
it's gotten better organized,
it has better names,

00:39:16.430 --> 00:39:18.620
it is in fact more
descriptive-- so it can

00:39:18.620 --> 00:39:21.290
describe a wider range of APIs.

00:39:21.290 --> 00:39:25.160
It has callback support in
it, which I think is fun,

00:39:25.160 --> 00:39:28.190
and the ability to use links to
connect operations and describe

00:39:28.190 --> 00:39:31.030
some of these sequences, and
then just general cleanup.

00:39:33.560 --> 00:39:36.470
If you want to get involved,
there's a bunch [INAUDIBLE]

00:39:36.470 --> 00:39:37.270
the beginning.

00:39:37.270 --> 00:39:39.020
We blogged about a lot
of the changes that

00:39:39.020 --> 00:39:42.757
were coming along the way
and then summarized those.

00:39:42.757 --> 00:39:44.840
Ron [? Rotoski ?] did a
couple of great blog posts

00:39:44.840 --> 00:39:46.370
recently about this, too.

00:39:46.370 --> 00:39:50.150
Darrel Miller was a big
help in this as well.

00:39:50.150 --> 00:39:53.300
Check out openapis.org.

00:39:53.300 --> 00:39:55.880
You can follow OpenAPI
spec on Twitter.

00:39:55.880 --> 00:39:57.590
That's where we
generally try to be

00:39:57.590 --> 00:39:58.964
proactive about
when we're having

00:39:58.964 --> 00:40:00.270
meet-ups and things like that.

00:40:00.270 --> 00:40:02.061
Actually, there's a
meet-up tomorrow night,

00:40:02.061 --> 00:40:05.750
I think, in San Francisco,
if you're interested.

00:40:05.750 --> 00:40:07.310
And then, like I
said, all of this

00:40:07.310 --> 00:40:08.768
is happening on
the OpenAPI branch.

00:40:08.768 --> 00:40:12.320
You can find two projects--
sorry, the OpenAPI [AUDIO OUT]

00:40:12.320 --> 00:40:13.010
GitHub.

00:40:13.010 --> 00:40:14.512
You will find two
projects there.

00:40:14.512 --> 00:40:15.470
One is the style guide.

00:40:15.470 --> 00:40:17.360
So if you were
looking for guidance

00:40:17.360 --> 00:40:19.700
on how to refer
to OpenAPI, either

00:40:19.700 --> 00:40:22.640
the spec or the initiative,
or get the logos,

00:40:22.640 --> 00:40:24.266
all of our source
logos are there

00:40:24.266 --> 00:40:25.640
with guidance on
how to use them.

00:40:28.860 --> 00:40:32.360
So third takeaway is
that the OpenAPI Version

00:40:32.360 --> 00:40:35.390
3, it's a big step forward,
it's generally an improvement.

00:40:35.390 --> 00:40:37.340
Ken [? Lane ?] did a
nice blog post recently

00:40:37.340 --> 00:40:40.487
on how pleased he was with
the evolution of this.

00:40:40.487 --> 00:40:43.070
That's exciting too because he
just joined the API Initiative.

00:40:43.070 --> 00:40:45.650
He's now on the governing board.

00:40:45.650 --> 00:40:47.900
This is a great opportunity
for tooling authors.

00:40:47.900 --> 00:40:50.450
So I would recommend
kicking the tires.

00:40:50.450 --> 00:40:54.680
See if the OpenAPI 3
spec serves your needs.

00:40:54.680 --> 00:40:57.530
Or if you see tooling that
needs to exist-- like right now,

00:40:57.530 --> 00:40:59.030
one of the next
things we have to do

00:40:59.030 --> 00:41:02.730
is to actually update the JSON
schema that describes the specs

00:41:02.730 --> 00:41:04.730
so we can validate specs.

00:41:04.730 --> 00:41:06.890
That's the stage we're
at, and we definitely

00:41:06.890 --> 00:41:09.184
need and will appreciate
help, even if it's just

00:41:09.184 --> 00:41:10.850
commentary on things
you liked or didn't

00:41:10.850 --> 00:41:14.150
like about the changes
that have been made.

00:41:14.150 --> 00:41:17.500
[MUSIC PLAYING]

